WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.440
By the end of Bloodshot, Ray Garrison is finally
starting to piece together his past — his

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:05.180
real past.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.550
Who was Ray before he became Bloodshot?

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:08.900
How did he die in the first place?

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.820
And what's this all building to, anyway?

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.180
We've got the answers — well, most of them,
anyway.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:17.520
In Bloodshot, most of what we learn about
Ray Garrison simply isn't true.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.080
The daring hostage rescue that opens the film
is a fabricated "hero module" designed to

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:22.860
give him confidence.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.860
His relationship with Gina ended five years
ago.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:26.900
She wasn't killed.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:27.620
She wasn't even his wife.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.880
"They've been manipulating you."

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.120
So, by the end of the movie, what do we know
about him?

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.640
Well, going off of a few stray lines of dialogue,
we can safely say that Ray really was part

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:37.640
of the military.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.320
Gina — the real one — notes that Ray kept
leaving on classified missions.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.620
He might've come home every time, but Gina
couldn't deal with him leaving in the first

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:45.400
place.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.560
That's why their relationship ended.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.160
We also know that Ray has a keen sense of
justice, a righteous temper, and doesn't let

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:51.760
go of a grudge.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.640
Bloodshot's whole thesis, which is summed
up at the end of the movie, is that memories

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.100
don't make the man.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:57.680
Actions do.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.190
As such, there's no reason to look backwards.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:03.879
The specifics of Ray's past might remain a
mystery, but it doesn't matter.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:05.060
He's moving forward regardless.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.580
For such a brutal story, Bloodshot has a surprisingly
uplifting ending.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.280
The bad guys are dead.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.060
Gina is safe and has moved on.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.090
Bloodshot's biggest limitation — the way
his nanites ran out of power, forcing him

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.720
to stop and recharge — has been removed.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.460
Now, it's just Ray, KT, Wilfred Wigans, their
shared trailer, and a beautiful oceanside

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:23.020
paradise.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.200
It's perfect — maybe a little too perfect.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.970
RST's entire scheme hinged on its ability
to manipulate Ray's reality.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:32.520
Who's to say they're not doing it again?

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.640
That's what Wilfred wonders right before the
film smash cuts to the credits.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.980
It's what KT implies when she says that their
tropical surroundings are a dream.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.560
Wilfred has a new, very different set of clothes.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.980
The final scene's warm lights and bright color
palette recall Ray and Gina's trip to Italy,

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.160
which was also a fantasy invented by RST.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.100
Now, this isn't Inception.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.400
Bloodshot isn't a particularly subtle movie,
and we're probably supposed to take everything

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.080
we see at face value.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.659
Still, if Sony wanted to open a Bloodshot
sequel by revealing that this happy ending

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:04.500
wasn't so happy at all, it wouldn't be a surprise.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.040
The groundwork has already been laid.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:10.060
"What you think is real, sometimes ain't.”

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.770
For all intents and purposes, Bloodshot is
married.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:17.000
It's the fictional death of his fake wife,
Gina, that spurs Bloodshot into action.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:21.490
Without that connection, false as it might
be, RST's whole plan for manipulating Ray

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:22.200
falls apart.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.300
And yet, if you thought you picked up on some
sexual tension between Ray and KT in the film's

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.420
final scene, you're not alone.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.900
KT totally has the hots for Ray, and it seems
like Ray is starting to feel the same way.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:37.190
Because of the movie's premise — KT could
fall in love with Ray over time, but not the

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:40.500
other way around — their romance had to
take an unusual form.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.700
As director Dave Wilson told us,

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.640
"Because of Bloodshot's total memory loss,
this could never be a conventional love relationship.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.920
I wanted a love story that the audience didn't
see coming."

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:53.080
Of course, the movie drops plenty of hints
that Bloodshot and KT are going to end up

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:53.660
as an item.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.420
When a devastated Ray learns that nobody came
to collect his body, KT reassures him that

00:02:58.580 --> 00:02:59.840
there's someone out there who loves him.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:04.780
At the time, you're supposed to think KT is
referring to Gina, who's still alive, but

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:05.920
no — she's talking about herself.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.280
While the movie's ending doesn't reveal Bloodshot's
identity, his comics do.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:11.840
Well, kind of.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.980
Fittingly, Bloodshot's backstory has been
rebooted and retconned many times since the

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:20.410
character's 1992 debut, and it's hard to pin
down an exact origin story.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:21.400
Ironic, right?

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.480
At least poor Ray comes by it honestly.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.560
In the first volume of Bloodshot, which was
originally written by Kevin VanHook and drawn

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:32.700
by Don Perlin, the titular hero was revealed
to be a violent mobster named Angelo Mortalli.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.460
Ruthless and driven, Mortalli was a rising
star in Don Gino Canelli's crime family until

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:40.942
Canelli learned that Mortalli was cheating
on his fiancee — who just happened to be

00:03:40.942 --> 00:03:41.580
Canelli's daughter.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.320
Canelli framed Mortalli for a violent crime,
forcing Mortalli into witness protection,

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:48.520
where he was killed by a corrupt FBI agent.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:51.880
Rising Spirit got its hands on him shortly
thereafter.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:56.901
The name Ray Garrison didn't enter Bloodshot
canon until volume two launched in 1997, as

00:03:56.901 --> 00:03:58.800
one of Bloodshot's other alternate identities.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.360
Over the years, he's had plenty of others.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:06.060
These days, Bloodshot assumes that "Ray" is
his real personality and that Angelo was a

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:10.330
cover he adopted while on a classified mission,
although he doesn't know for sure.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:11.800
At this rate, he never will.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:17.160
Here's the thing, though: The cinematic Bloodshot
could still go the Angelo Mortalli route,

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.260
assuming that the movie gets a sequel.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:24.000
According to former Valiant Entertainment
CEO Dinesh Shamdasani, early drafts of the

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.900
Bloodshot script leaned heavily into the mafia
angle, and the film's lack of definite answers

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.920
leaves room for Mortalli to appear in the
sequel.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.760
In fact, we already have one hint that Bloodshot's
ending is a little deceptive, and that Ray

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.640
— or whoever he actually is — isn't exactly
the stand-up guy the finale makes him out

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:39.180
to be.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.260
During one of Bloodshot's action scenes, Dr.
Harting taunts Ray by noting that the soldier

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.080
signed up for military service because he
enjoys violence.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.280
Ray protests, but the evidence is all there
on screen.

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:53.930
Let's face it: Hijacking an airplane and killing
half a dozen people isn't exactly a normal

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:55.100
response to trauma.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.040
"You know nothing about men like me!"

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.300
It's an interesting concept, and one that
could make for an interesting angle in a sequel.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:05.130
By the end of the movie, Bloodshot says he's
done looking backwards, but learning that

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:08.880
he was more of a villain than a hero pre-transformation
could make him change his mind.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:14.100
Of all the different iterations of Bloodshot,
the film draws the most heavily on the 2012

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.040
reboot, which really played up the entire
false memories and mind control angles.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:22.660
In that story, Bloodshot learns that he's
being manipulated fairly early on, and takes

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:24.720
the war to Rising Spirit fairly quickly.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:29.030
That's where the movie ends, but if Bloodshot's
sequels continue down that path, then we know

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:30.760
exactly where this story is going.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.740
In the comics, Bloodshot learns he's not Project
Rising Spirit's only victim.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:38.920
Over the years, the project has also been
kidnapping children with superpowers, who

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:39.800
are known as harbingers.

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:44.460
By making Bloodshot think that the kids are
in danger, Rising Spirit has been relying

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:46.880
on the supersoldier to round up the powerful
little tykes.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:51.210
Bloodshot drops the kids off at the project's
labs, where they're caged and experimented

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:54.100
on, then has his memory wiped before he sets
off for the next batch.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:58.670
Naturally, Bloodshot frees the kids when he
learns what's going on, but dropping two dozen

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:02.860
super-powered children into the real world
without any warning has big repercussions.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.900
In the comics it led to a massive crossover
event called "Harbinger Wars."

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.680
In theaters, it could lead to a similar, Avengers-style
event — if one major obstacle doesn't get

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:12.260
in the way.

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.400
Originally, Harbinger Wars was Valiant and
Sony's endgame.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:21.900
In 2015, The Hollywood Reporter confirmed
that Bloodshot — then scheduled for a 2017

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.660
release, with John Wick's Chad Stahelski and
David Leitch set to direct — was going to

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:28.420
be the first entry in Valiant's big-screen
superhero universe.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:30.380
Harbinger would be the second.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:34.670
Each movie would get a sequel, and then the
two franchises would crossover for the Harbinger

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:35.220
Wars event.

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:39.020
However, in 2019, the Harbinger movie moved
from Sony to Paramount.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:44.160
With the characters now at two separate companies,
a crossover seems increasingly unlikely.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.900
It could still happen, of course — Sony
itself has already teamed up with Marvel Studios

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:53.120
to bring Spider-Man into the MCU, although
that relationship has been relatively complicated

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:54.700
— but the chances aren't great.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:59.220
When we asked Bloodshot director Dave Wilson
about a potential sequel, he was quick to

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:01.620
list all the places a larger Bloodshot universe
could go.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:06.060
"There are groups and characters that I love,
from Bloodsquirt to Bloodhound to H.A.R.D.

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:06.740
Corps.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.450
There is enough material out there in the
Valiant universe to make any number of films

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:10.020
you want."

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:14.280
Tellingly, though, while those characters
do interact with the larger Valiant universe,

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.760
they're primarily Bloodshot spinoffs.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:20.110
At this point in time, a larger Valiant series
with Bloodshot at the center seems like a

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:21.200
fairly dicey proposition.

